            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager_smp.cpp",
                    "line": 380,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "Just curious. Why doesn't starting this target cause all chassis to be powered off, eliminating the need for line 383?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager_smp.cpp",
                    "line": 380,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "The main way to make that happen would be to Add obmc-chassis-poweroff[1-N].target into the obmc-chassis-poweroff@0.target.requires. I thought about that initially as a way to avoid needed this SMP app, but as I responded to Patrick's question, there were a lot of complexities and corner cases with that. There could be some optimizations here to not call the chassis which is already powering off but for testing simplicity, it's nice to just go down the common path of the full requestTransitionOnAllChassis() path."
                }
            ],
